{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>And he did not know and is guilty, and he shall bring.</b> This matter speaks of one into whose hand comes a doubtful case of karet and he does not know whether he transgressed it or not — for example, forbidden fat and permitted suet before him, and he thought both were permitted and ate one. They said to him: one of them was forbidden fat, and he does not know whether the one he ate was the forbidden fat — this one brings an asham talui, and it protects him all the while it is not known to him that he definitely sinned. And if it becomes known to him after some time, he brings a hatat (Keritot 22)." "<b>And he did not know and is guilty and bears his iniquity.</b> Rabbi Yosei HaGelili says: Scripture punished one who did not know — how much more so would it punish one who did know. Rabbi Yosei says: If you wish to know the reward of the righteous, go and learn from the first man, who was commanded only a negative commandment and transgressed it — see how many deaths were decreed upon him and upon his generations. And which measure is greater — that of good or that of punishment? You must say: the measure of good. If the lesser measure of punishment — see how many deaths were decreed upon him and upon his generations; the greater measure of good that accrues to him from the piggulin and the notarot and from fasting on Yom HaKippurim — how much more so that it should earn merit for him and for his generations and for the generations of his generations to the end of all generations. Rabbi Akiva says: Scripture says ״עַל פִּי שְׁנַיִם עֵדִים אוֹ שְׁלֹשָׁה״ (Deuteronomy 17) — if testimony is established by two, why does Scripture specify three? Rather, to bring the third one for stringency, and to treat his law like those others regarding the punishment of false testimony. If so, Scripture punished the one who merely attaches himself to transgressors as though he were a transgressor — how much more so will it pay good reward to the one who attaches himself to doers of commandments as though he were a doer of commandments (Sanhedrin 9). Rabbi Elazar ben Azarya says: ״כִּי תִקְצֹר קְצִירְךָ בְשָׂדֶךָ וְשָׁכַחְתָּ עֹמֶר בַּשָּׂדֶה״ — Scripture says ״לְמַעַן יְבָרֶכְךָ״ (Deuteronomy 24). Scripture established a blessing for one through whose hand a commandment comes without his knowledge. Say from now on: a sela was tied in his corner-fold, and it fell from him, and a poor person found it and was sustained by it — the Holy One, blessed be He, establishes a blessing for him (Sifra)."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:05:13.169063621Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}